traceability.evidence:
Story file existence: docs/stories/022.0-DEV-JSDOC-COEXISTENCE.story.md is present (validated via check_file_exists).,Tests explicitly tied to this story and its requirements: - tests/rules/valid-annotation-format.test.ts header: "* Tests for: docs/stories/022.0-DEV-JSDOC-COEXISTENCE.story.md
 * @story docs/stories/022.0-DEV-JSDOC-COEXISTENCE.story.md
 * @req REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE ... REQ-ANNOTATION-TERMINATION ... REQ-JSDOC-BOUNDARY-DETECTION ... REQ-CONTINUATION-LOGIC ... REQ-NO-FALSE-POSITIVES ... REQ-PRESERVE-MULTILINE". - Jest run (npm test -- --verbose) shows this suite passing with the REQ IDs in names: - "[REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE] traceability before other JSDoc tags" - "[REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE] traceability after other JSDoc tags" - "[REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE] mixed positions of traceability and other JSDoc tags" - "[REQ-PRESERVE-MULTILINE] multi-line @story annotation before other JSDoc tags" - "[REQ-NO-FALSE-POSITIVES] JSDoc tags do not pollute requirement ID when following @req",Acceptance criteria mapping to concrete tests (all in tests/rules/valid-annotation-format.test.ts, all passing per Jest output): - Before Other Tags: valid case name: "[REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE] traceability before other JSDoc tags", code JSDoc with @story and @req followed by @param and @returns. - After Other Tags: valid case name: "[REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE] traceability after other JSDoc tags", code JSDoc with @param/@returns first, then @story and @req. - Mixed Positions: valid case name: "[REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE] mixed positions of traceability and other JSDoc tags", code with @story, then @param, then @req, then another @param, @returns, and a second @req. - Continuation & multiline preservation: valid case name: "[REQ-PRESERVE-MULTILINE] multi-line @story annotation before other JSDoc tags", where the @story value is split across two lines before @param/@returns. - No false positives for JSDoc lines after @req: valid case name: "[REQ-NO-FALSE-POSITIVES] JSDoc tags do not pollute requirement ID when following @req", reproduces the original bug scenario and now passes (no invalid ID like "REQ-OPTIMIZATION@param{object}data").,Core implementation of JSDoc coexistence behavior in rule logic: - src/rules/valid-annotation-format.ts, function processCommentLine: - Detects @story, @req, @supports and starts/validates annotations as before. - New JSDoc coexistence logic: "// Implement JSDoc tag coexistence behavior: terminate @story/@req values when a new non-traceability JSDoc tag line (e.g., @param, @returns) is encountered.
 // @supports docs/stories/022.0-DEV-JSDOC-COEXISTENCE.story.md REQ-ANNOTATION-TERMINATION REQ-CONTINUATION-LOGIC
 if (isNonTraceabilityJSDocTagLine(normalized)) {
 finalizePendingAnnotation(context, comment, options, pending);
 return null;
 }" - This ensures annotation values are properly terminated at JSDoc tag boundaries and that continuation only happens on non-tag lines.,Boundary detection and normalization helpers updated for this story: - src/rules/helpers/valid-annotation-format-internal.ts: - normalizeCommentLine(rawLine: string): * Trims each raw line and strips a leading "*" for JSDoc-style lines. * Detects @story/@req/@supports and returns the substring starting at the tag, but crucially does not join multiple physical lines; multi-line behavior is handled in processCommentLine, preserving line boundaries as required by REQ-ANNOTATION-TERMINATION and REQ-PRESERVE-MULTILINE. - isNonTraceabilityJSDocTagLine(normalized: string): "const trimmed = normalized.trimStart();
 if (!trimmed || !trimmed.startsWith("@")) return false;
 if (/^@(story|req|supports) /.test(trimmed)) return false;
 return true;" * This matches any JSDoc-style tag starting with "@" except the traceability ones and is used in processCommentLine to terminate pending @story/@req values when a non-traceability JSDoc tag line (e.g. @param, @returns, @throws) appears. This satisfies REQ-JSDOC-BOUNDARY-DETECTION and the "JSDoc Tag Detection" acceptance criterion.,Continuation logic behavior (REQ-CONTINUATION-LOGIC / continuation acceptance criteria): - In processCommentLine (src/rules/valid-annotation-format.ts): * The order of checks is: detect @story/@req/@supports, then detect isNonTraceabilityJSDocTagLine, and only if none match and pending != null does it treat the line as a continuation. * This means any line starting with "@" becomes either a traceability annotation (starting a new pending value) or a JSDoc boundary (terminating the pending), and is never used as a continuation. Lines without leading "@" (after normalization) are concatenated to pending.value. This exactly matches "Parser only continues multi-line values when line doesn't start with @tag".,Backward compatibility and error-message behavior: - The valid-annotation-format rule still has extensive invalid cases for story 005.0, 007.0, 010.1, 010.2 (missing story path, invalid extensions, invalid req formats, invalid @supports formats, invalid regex configs, etc.). - Jest verbose output shows all these invalid tests passing with the expected messages, indicating existing functionality and error messages are preserved (satisfying Backward Compatible and Error Messages criteria). - No regressions are indicated: the test summary shows "Test Suites: 36 passed, 36 total; Tests: 282 passed, 282 total".,Documentation updated for JSDoc coexistence, referencing this story: - docs/rules/valid-annotation-format.md contains a dedicated **JSDoc coexistence** section: - Describes that traceability annotations can appear before, after, or interleaved with other JSDoc tags. - States: "The parser treats any JSDoc tag line that starts with `@` (other than `@story`, `@req`, or `@supports`) as a boundary that terminates the current multi-line traceability value" and that continuation lines only continue when they do not start with another JSDoc tag. - Provides an example using docs/stories/022.0-DEV-JSDOC-COEXISTENCE.story.md with @story/@req followed by @param and @returns. - This satisfies the "Documentation" acceptance criterion and directly describes REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE, REQ-ANNOTATION-TERMINATION, REQ-JSDOC-BOUNDARY-DETECTION, REQ-CONTINUATION-LOGIC, REQ-NO-FALSE-POSITIVES, and REQ-PRESERVE-MULTILINE.,End-to-end verification via ESLint rule tests: - The valid-annotation-format rule is exercised via RuleTester (tests/rules/valid-annotation-format.test.ts) with realistic code snippets including functions and JSDoc blocks, not just isolated lines. - The Jest run (npm test -- --verbose) shows all these tests as passing, confirming the implemented parsing logic behaves as specified for JSDoc coexistence and does not break other rule behavior.
